  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Showers and scattered strong to potentially severe\nthunderstorms, with the threat for localized gusty to damaging\nwinds for late today and early tonight, mainly across the NW\nthird of PA. \n\nIncreasingly southwesterly flow at the surface and aloft will \ncarry warm and humid air into the region today, in advance of a\ncold front moving southeast across the Upper Great Lakes. An \naxis of high PWAT air (1.75\"+) will reach the south shore of \nLake Erie during the early afternoon, then sag SE across the NW \nMtns early this evening before reaching the Southern Tier of PA \nbefore daybreak Thursday. \n\nThis surge of moisture, coupled with clearing in West/Central\nPA, has allowed for sufficient instability to develop for \nstorms to feed on in this evening. Latest RAP Mesoanalysis has \n2000+ SBCAPE J/kg nosing its way into Western PA and 1000+ J/kg \nin the western half of the state. Lapse rates aloft aren't very \nimpressive though, leading to tall but skinny CAPE profiles \nexpected this evening. This favors heavy, efficient \nprecipitation and coupled with Corfidi Vectors expected in the \n10-15kt range could lead to training storms. Isolated flash \nflooding in Northwest PA is possible as a result, but fast \nenough storm speed should preclude a more widespread threat.\n\nThe main deep layer shear and upper level forcing though \nremains in Southern Michigan as of 18Z and is still a few hours\naway from Northwest PA. This leads to uncertainty regarding \nstorm coverage and degree of organization. Should these features\nalign with the best CAPE this evening, greater coverage and \nmore organization of storms is more likely. A jet developing at\n850mb this evening, coupled with forward storm speed and\nprecipitation loading in downdrafts, will allow for damaging\nwind gusts to be the main hazard. Low level shear from this 850mb\njet will allow for 0-1km and 0-3km shear to be in the 20-30kt\nrange this evening. Coupled with cyclonic low level hodograph \ncurvature, a transient tornado threat may emerge from embedded \nsupercell structures and/or meso-vorticies depending on the \ndominant storm mode. Day cloud phase has shown an area of \nenhanced cumulus clouds this afternoon in the northern PA \ncounties. This indicates a potential low level boundary present,\nwhich may act as a focus for storms and source of extra \nvorticity for storms to ingest. Storms are anticipated to weaken\nas they push further south and east tonight with increasingly \nunfavorable instability. However, they will still be capable of \nproducing localized heavy downpours. \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Showers confined to southern tier of PA \nThursday. Dry conditions Friday before unsettled weekend. \n\nThe most recent WPC ERO has pushed most of the Day 2 Marginal \nto our south. Some lingering precipitation in the early morning\nmay occur in the southern tier as the previous night's wave\npushes through. The main shot of precipitation tomorrow is most\nlikely in the southern tier, particularly in the southwest, as\nthe cold front moves through in the afternoon. Most 6hr HREF \nPMMs stay below 1\" tomorrow afternoon and evening, but some\nlocally heavy rain remains possible. \n\nHigh pressure builds behind the cold front, giving way to a dry\nand sunny Friday. Low level moisture lurking just south of the \nMason/Dixon line Fri night will advance NE across the state on \nSaturday as SFC high pressure moves well off the Mid Atlantic \nand Southern New England Coast. This will lead to some showers \nspreading NE across the region with a few PM TSRA possible \nSaturday and/or Sunday afternoons, especially over the western \nmtns. A more- active (wetter) pattern is in store for Mon-Tues. \nBut, the flat upper ridge over the southern half of the CONUS \nwill make it difficult for deep moisture to make it into PA \nahead of any shots of atmos forcing. The second half of the week\nprobably ends dry as the upper ridge pumps up over the central \nCONUS.",
